if you notice we're all wearing red  today because it's Go Red For Women day  part of a month-long national campaign  to raise awareness for women's heart  health heart disease is the leading  cause of death for American women it  kills about 300,000 each year but a new  survey finds that less than half of  women are aware of the danger about 60%  of women don't know critical components  of their cardiac health like their  cholesterol numbers blood sugar levels  or body mass index our doctor Taryn Rula  is a cardiologist at northwell health  good morning good morning  heart disease still the leading cause of  death for men and women in America why  are women at such a high risk I think  there's two things going on the first is  as a medical community we have  understudied women we have under  diagnosed them and we have under treated  them they don't get the same medical or  procedural treatments that men receive  in addition there's obviously an  awareness problem and the Go Red For  Women campaign was started in 2004 has  done a lot but we're still not where we  need to be women do not recognize that  this is their biggest threat they don't  recognize the risk factors and how to  manage them they don't recognize  symptoms and I think one of the main  problems and we were discussing this is  it's not personal enough to women one of  the best things that go Red For Women  campaign has done is put faces to this  disease if you go to the website it says  real women real stories and I think when  you see the face of a 40 year old woman  who says I was fit and exercising and I  had a heart attack or a 35 year old  woman who had a stroke suddenly it  becomes personal and not just a  statistic that one woman dies every  minute it means more yeah I feel it's  very personal we ask people to go on  Facebook and Twitter you know both my  parents died of heart attack and heart  attacks and I sit here today at the age  of 63 I've outlived them both so I take  it very very seriously but on Facebook  Marie I relate to you she said I just  turned 40 I have a strong history of  heart disease on my mother's side of the  family what should I be doing now and  what should I be discussing because is  it genetic I always think that it is it  can be and it's great that she knows she  has a family history that's one thing we  tell people is ask your family members  when did they get diagnosed what did  they die from you need to have that  information and bring it to your doctor  well once you know that in a way it's  almost like the deck is stacked a little  against you so you have to be even more  aggressive about managing your risk  factors so what you need to do is talk  to your doctor about your numbers you  need to know  your blood pressure your cholesterol  your body mass index your fasting blood  sugar need to be armed with that  information and then have your doctor  tell you what your risk is of  cardiovascular disease and work with you  on managing your risk factors what are  some of the risk factors so main risk  factors that we always talk about  hypertension hypercholesterolemia  smoking being overweight but then women  don't think about things like migraine  polycystic ovarian syndrome lupus or  rheumatoid arthritis early menopause  some of these can be risk factors too  dr. taryn are all really good  information thank you so much and Tara  will answer your questions about heart  health in a live facebook chat this  morning so you can take part by visiting  our CBS this morning Facebook page  it's expected to start around 10:15 and  yes you can get some free medical advice  that looks like that
 

